arXiv:2510.05577v1 [cs.CL] 07 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dong Yan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Central South University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yandong203519@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gaochen Wu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Tsinghua University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bowen Zhou
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding Author</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Tsinghua University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Shanghai AI Laboratory
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in language agents have led to significant improvements in multi-hop reasoning tasks. However, existing approaches often struggle with handling open-domain problems, which require massive information retrieval due to their reliance on a fixed sequence of actions. To address this, we propose Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning (FGDIP), a novel framework tailored to enhance reasoning in LLMs by utilizing dynamic and adaptive strategies for information exploration in open-domain multi-hop reasoning tasks. Our approach begins by identifying key entities relevant to the problem, which serve as the initial nodes in the reasoning process. From these initial nodes, we then generate reasoning child nodes with the process being refined through a combination of historical error analysis and real-time feedback, which allows the framework to dynamically adjust and optimize its reasoning strategies. By integrating depth-first search with an innovative node generation technique, our framework adapts based on both prior error paths and concurrently generated nodes at the same hierarchical level. This dynamic strategy effectively expands the search space while ensuring the reasoning process systematically converges toward accurate solutions.
Experimental results show that FGDIP achieved up to 54.47% F1 score on the HotpotQA dataset and 70.05% on the StrategyQA dataset, surpassing the best baseline by 5.03% and 7.25% respectively, highlighting its versatility and potential to enhance language agents in multi-hop reasoning tasks.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05577v1/liuchengtu.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the FGDIP framework. It initiates by identifying key entities related to the problem and utilizes a depth-first search approach for thorough exploration of each node. The process draws insights from both error antecedent branches and concurrently generated nodes at the same hierarchical level, thus facilitating the refinement and optimization of subsequent exploration.The Step Evaluator assesses the feasibility of each newly generated node, provides a detailed rationale, and then ranks nodes to prioritize the most promising paths. Answer Evaluator assesses the results for relevance, integrating any error analysis to enhance future exploration.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The emergence of language agents marks a significant milestone in the evolution of artificial intelligence technologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Built on robust LLM frameworks like GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, these agents handle complex tasks by generating real-time strategies. Recent research emphasizes improvements in task planning and execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> through stepwise problem-solving. Studies have explored adaptive learning techniques, which are crucial for agents to reflect on past interactions, learn from experiences, and improve future performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Moreover, collaboration among multiple agents has been enhanced, allowing for the division of tasks among specialized systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>. Integrating external tools has expanded language agents’ scope, enabling access to diverse information and computational resources for nuanced responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although these methods demonstrate notable effectiveness, they remain challenged in open-domain tasks such as HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, where dynamic information retrieval and data filtering play a pivotal role in supporting multi-hop question answering. In addressing multi-hop reasoning challenges, prevalent methodologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that utilize ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> paradigm for sequential execution demonstrate inherent limitations. Initially, these models perform a breakdown of the problem, establishing a retrieval sequence that rigidly guides subsequent searches. This linear, token-by-token decision-making process, once initiated, constrains the model’s ability to dynamically adapt its strategy based on evolving information. When essential contexts are missing during search iterations, models are more likely to deem a solution unattainable or repeatedly search the same keywords until they exhaust all attempts. These limitations constrain the search space of these methods when solving open domain problems. Additionally, current approaches in natural language processing often involve fine-tuning language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which is computationally expensive and data-intensive.

<br class="ltx_break"> Motivated by the need for effective multi-hop reasoning in open-domain problems, we propose FGDIP, a novel framework that dynamically adapts its strategies for information exploration without requiring any model fine-tuning. As shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the model begins by identifying key entities relevant to the problem, which serve as the initial nodes in the reasoning process. Each node represents distinct thought, action, and observation step. The model employs a depth-first search strategy to explore each initial node thoroughly. During this process, newly expanded nodes incorporate insights from both error antecedent branches and concurrently generated nodes at the same hierarchical level, which ensures comprehensive exploration of the solution space and continuous refinement of strategies towards converging on the correct answer. The Step Evaluator assesses the feasibility of each newly generated node, provides a detailed rationale, and then ranks the node among others to prioritize the most promising paths. Finally, upon deriving a result, Answer Evaluator examines the result to determine its relevance to the topic. If the result is deemed on-topic, the process concludes; otherwise the experience from erroneous judgments is integrated for future reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our experimental evaluation reveals that FGDIP significantly surpasses existing baselines on both the HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and StrategyQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al., 2021</a>)</cite> datasets. On HotpotQA, FGDIP achieves 60.46% F1 score on easy questions, 53.87% on medium questions, and 48.56% on hard questions, representing improvements of 3.08%, 1.80%, and 8.66% respectively over the best baseline. On StrategyQA, FGDIP achieves an F1 score of 70.05%, outperforming UALA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite> by 7.25%. Additionally, FGDIP also demonstrates strong performance on the Game of 24 dataset, a mathematical reasoning challenge regarded as a closed-domain task. These results demonstrate FGDIP’s superior ability to dynamically adjust its reasoning strategies and effectively handle complex, multi-hop, and implicit reasoning tasks. The main contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose FGDIP, a novel framework for solving open-domain problems that require multi-hop reasoning, which expands the scope of information search and employs more flexible and adaptive strategies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a method in which each step of the decision-making process leverages the accumulation of historical error analysis and a comprehensive real-time action feedback. This approach enables the model to initially diverge and subsequently converge on the correct answer throughout the multi-hop reasoning process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct comprehensive experiments of our framework, demonstrating that FGDIP can improve the downstream performance of multi-hop reasoning tasks in both open and closed domain tasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Language Agents</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As the field of language agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> advances, various strategies have emerged to leverage their reasoning capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> combines reasoning and action to tackle complex tasks, adapting to real-time interactions. Reflexion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite> enhances language agents through verbal feedback, avoiding traditional weight updates. Chameleon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> offers a flexible framework that equips LLMs with diverse tools for compositional reasoning across a wide range of problems. Dr3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> offers a systematic approach to detecting and rectifying off-topic answers through dynamic reasoning refinement in multi-hop question answering. The KnowAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> framework enhances task planning and execution using a knowledge-augmented approach to minimize planning hallucinations and improve action coherence. UALA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduce a language agent framework that leverages uncertainty measurement to refine reasoning processes and optimize external resource utilization. Unlike their work, our approach involves more comprehensive and adaptive strategies designed to enhance the exploration of information, enabling the model to dynamically adjust its strategies based on available context, rather than being constrained to a fixed sequence of actions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Multi-Step Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in large language models have highlighted the efficacy of structured prompting techniques for enhanced reasoning. The Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite> approach enables LLMs to tackle complex problems by sequentially following thought processes. The Self-Consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> strategy builds on this by generating multiple reasoning chains and selecting the most consistent one. The Tree of Thoughts model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite> further advances the chain-of-thought approach by allowing LLMs to explore multiple reasoning paths within a structured framework. Given its high capabilities, the foundational architecture of our approach extensively employs the ToT structure. Nevertheless, our architecture transcends the conventional ToT framework. In the generation of new nodes, our approach systematically incorporates insights from both antecedent branches and concurrently generated nodes at the same hierarchical level, thereby facilitating graph-like interconnections among the nodes. Additionally, the Graph of Thoughts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Besta et al., 2024</a>)</cite> framework structures thoughts in a graph format to support intricate and interdependent reasoning, fostering a robust mechanism for solving more elaborate problems. The majority of existing studies focus on multi-hop reasoning within well-defined, closed problem spaces. Conversely, our research is directed towards addressing open-ended problems that necessitate extensive exploration and creative thinking and our methodological approach systematically narrows down potential solutions through a progressive refinement during the exploration process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05577v1/casestudy.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/298;" width="476" height="298"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Comparison between ReAct paradigm and FGDIP. The ReAct method failed due to vague keyword use like "Charles Fort publishing company" and didn’t adapt when initial searches were unsuccessful. It also incorrectly identified only a temporary publisher for "Fortean Times" without confirming the publisher for "Bizarre." In contrast, FGDIP efficiently switched to "Bizarre magazine" after initial searches failed, quickly identifying Dennis Publishing as the common publisher for both "Bizarre" and "Fortean Times," demonstrating its precision in query processing.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Multivariate Information Extractor</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Multivariate Information Extractor module is designed to identify and extract key entities that are critical for solving complex problems. It starts by analyzing the problem statement to determine which entities are specific and unique, ensuring that the problem is thoroughly understood from multiple dimensions. These extracted entities, serving as the first layer of nodes, facilitate a more targeted and effective strategy for further reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional models addressing complex queries with multiple entities often follow a rigid sequence of actions, focusing initially on the most obvious entity and attempting to gather all necessary information linearly. This approach can fail if the entity lacks all the relevant data needed for resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, our method avoids such pitfalls by not committing to a fixed procedural pathway from the outset. After the first attempt proves insufficient for solving the query, our model shifts to exploring alternative entities. By doing so, it opens up new avenues for inquiry that are more likely to lead to successful problem resolution. This iterative, entity-focused strategy enhances the model’s ability to handle complex, multi-faceted queries more effectively, ensuring a higher rate of successful information retrieval and problem-solving.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Node Generator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method employs adaptive candidate generation, leveraging historical error analysis and real-time feedback to optimize multi-hop reasoning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Error Analysis</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the decision-making process and improve accuracy, our method meticulously maintains a record of past errors resulting from Answer Evaluator, which will be introduced in Section <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS2" title="3.3.2 Answer Evaluator ‣ 3.3 Evaluator ‣ 3 Method ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.2</span></a>. The insights derived from these errors, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1\dots i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1\dots i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, are leveraged to guide and refine future decision-making steps. This historical analysis ensures that the model learns from previous mistakes, thereby preventing the repetition of ineffective strategies and enhancing the overall decision accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Real-time Feedback</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to historical analysis, real-time feedback plays a crucial role during node expansion. As the model explores new nodes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, it continuously evaluates the real-time feedback from the interactions with concurrently generated nodes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{ij}^{{}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ij}^{{}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the current layer. This integration is essential for maximizing the utility of the most recent observations, ensuring that useful information is effectively utilized and redundant actions are minimized. The combination of leveraging past errors and real-time feedback ensures a more adaptive and accurate problem-solving approach, leading to a higher success rate in complex reasoning tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Node Generation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the insights from historical error analysis and real-time feedback integration, our method formalizes the generation of new nodes in the decision-making process. Specifically, when generating the j-th layer node <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> in the i-th branch, the node <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is influenced by:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The input question <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The historical error analysis <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1\ldots i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1\ldots i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the preceding branches.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Real-time feedback from interactions with other nodes <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{ij}^{{}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ij}^{{}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the current layer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The current branch’s trajectory <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i1,\ldots,i(j-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i1,\ldots,i(j-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The node generation approach is formally expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{ij}\sim p(z_{ij}\mid x,e_{1\ldots i-1},z_{ij}^{{}^{\prime}},z_{i1,\ldots,i(j-1)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ij}\sim p(z_{ij}\mid x,e_{1\ldots i-1},z_{ij}^{{}^{\prime}},z_{i1,\ldots,i(j-1)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Evaluator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2024)</a></cite>, our method enhances the evaluation process by introducing a detailed and explanatory framework for assessing both the feasibility of procedural steps and the correctness of answers. This improvement addresses the limitations of previous methods, which often relied on simple binary conclusions without providing reasoning behind the judgments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Step Evaluator</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When each node is generated, the Step Evaluator assesses potential success of the steps taken in the problem-solving process through a multi-dimensional approach:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comprehensive Step Analysis:</span> The evaluator reviews the entire trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i1,\ldots,ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i1,\ldots,ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, including the thoughts process, actions taken, and observations made.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feasibility Assessment:</span> It evaluates the likelihood that the sequence of steps will lead to the correct answer. This process can be formalized as evaluating the value function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(p,z_{i1,\ldots,ij})(z_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(p,z_{i1,\ldots,ij})(z_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> for the sequence of steps <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i1,\ldots,ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i1,\ldots,ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the current step <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is assessed according to the likelihood <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(v\mid z_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(v\mid z_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> of leading to a successful outcome:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(p,z_{i1,\ldots,ij})(z_{ij})\sim p(v\mid z_{ij})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(p,z_{i1,\ldots,ij})(z_{ij})\sim p(v\mid z_{ij}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detailed Justification:</span> Each judgment includes an analysis, explaining the likelihood of a step leading to the correct answer, enhancing the transparency and value of the model’s output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Categorization:</span> Based on comprehensive assessment, the feasibility of steps is categorized into levels "sure," "maybe," or "impossible", which is the scalar value <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Steps are then explored in order of priority, focusing on the most promising paths first.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Answer Evaluator</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Answer Evaluator leverages the capabilities of large language models to assess the relevance of generated result in relation to the original query. Unlike traditional methods that focus solely on correctness, which may result in a misalignment between the question and its generated answer (e.g. asking about the championship-winning team but receiving a response with the name of an individual from that team), the evaluator examines whether the generated result is on-topic by considering its alignment with the context and intent of the query. This process involves presenting the LLM with both the query and the generated result, prompting it to evaluate whether the generated result solves the problem and whether it is on-topic, and gives the reasons for the judgment. The integration of detailed reasoning in the Answer Evaluator promotes a deeper understanding of the model’s decisions, significantly improving the quality and reliability of the outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Explore Algorithm</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Explore Algorithm in our framework employs a depth-first search strategy to navigate through the solution space. This approach allows the algorithm to dive deep into each reasoning node, continuing until it reaches an endpoint or meets specific conditions, which aligns perfectly with our framework of constructing and verifying a complete reasoning chain.
Additionally, the DFS strategy’s deep exploration creates paths that are ideal for monitoring and adjustment by our Answer Evaluator and Error Analysis. If the Answer Evaluator identifies a path as invalid, the algorithm backtracks to explore other potential pathways. This systematic approach enables real-time identification and correction of erroneous paths, preventing the model from repeating the same mistakes and ensuring a more comprehensive and optimized exploration of the solution space. Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Multi-Step Reasoning ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides comparison between ReAct paradigm and FGDIP.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center">Method</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" colspan="4"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HotpotQA</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Easy</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Medium</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Hard</span>
</span></th>
