</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tasks.</span> We evaluate our model on HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and StrategyQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al., 2021</a>)</cite> datasets. HotpotQA requires multi-hop reasoning over Wikipedia passages with free-form answers. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we use their datasets which consist of questions divided into three levels for evaluation. StrategyQA is an open-domain dataset with binary answers, focusing on implicit reasoning where queries don’t explicitly guide the derivation of correct answers. We use the same dev set used in FireAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> to evaluate our model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We use GPT-3.5-Turbo, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Gemini-1.5-flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2023</a>)</cite> as the inference engines. We compare FGDIP with 1) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, a method utilizing the basic capabilities of model for inference with a few example prompts. 2) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-Thought</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a reasoning approach that mimics human thought patterns, allowing the model to explain its reasoning steps. 3) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReAct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, a paradigm that synergizes reasoning and action, enabling the model to tackle complex tasks by adapting to real-time interactions. 4) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflexion</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, applied to ReAct, incorporating ground truth label feedback to iteratively enhance performance by enabling the model to reflect on its previous outputs. 5) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dr3</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a mechanism that detects and corrects off-topic answers in large language models through iterative reasoning and backtracking. 6) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UALA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a language agent framework that manages interaction uncertainty to optimize reasoning and reduce external dependencies. We evaluate the performance of each method using the F1 score, which measures the agreement between the predicted and ground-truth answers, with the reward ranging from 0 to 1.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
