</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Explore Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and  <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> detail FGDIP’s performance against various baselines using GPT-3.5-Turbo, while Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Model Generalizability Experiments ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> demonstrates FGDIP’s generalizability across different large language models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HotpotQA Datasets Analysis.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Explore Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, FGDIP’s F1 scores across different difficulty levels are notably higher than the baselines. In particular, FGDIP achieves a 60.46% F1 score for easy questions, a 53.87% F1 score for medium questions, and a 48.56% F1 score for hard questions. These results represent substantial improvements of 3.08%, 1.80%, and 8.66% respectively compared to the best baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The primary reason for this improvement is FGDIP’s ability to dynamically adjust its reasoning process by integrating real-time feedback and historical error analysis, which allows it to navigate complex, multi-hop questions more effectively than static methods like Chain-of-Thought.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:110.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05577v1/noextractor.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:179/91;" width="179" height="91"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Without Extractor</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:110.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05577v1/nostepevaluator.svg" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:179/91;" width="179" height="91"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Without Step Evaluator</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:110.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05577v1/noanswerevaluator.svg" id="S4.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:179/91;" width="179" height="91"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Without Answer Evaluator</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Performance analysis of model components on F1 Score: (a) Framework without the Extractor module, illustrating the impact of omitting the component responsible for breaking down complex queries, (b) Framework without the Step Evaluator, showing effective in maintaining performance while minimizing resource consumption, and (c) Framework without the Answer Evaluator, showing the outcomes of excluding the module for iterative answer validation and correction.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">StrategyQA Datasets Analysis.</span> In the StrategyQA dataset, as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, FGDIP achieves an F1 score of 70.05%, outperforming the best baseline UALA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which had an F1 score of 62.80%. The 7.25% improvement is due to FGDIP’s iterative approach that breaks down complex reasoning tasks and refines strategies based on real-time feedback and past errors. This is crucial for StrategyQA, where indirect reasoning challenges static methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance Breakdown and Reasons.</span> The detailed performance breakdown reveals that FGDIP’s advantage grows with the complexity of the questions. For difficult questions that require multiple reasoning steps, the dynamic adaptation of FGDIP allows it to explore a broader search space and identify more relevant information paths, leading to more accurate answers. This is particularly evident in HotpotQA’s hard question category, where FGDIP outperforms the baseline by 8.66%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, for simpler questions where the reasoning path is straightforward, the performance gap between FGDIP and the baselines is narrower. This indicates that the benefits of FGDIP’s dynamic and adaptive strategy are maximized in scenarios where complex reasoning and flexible information exploration are required.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Analysis</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze the effectiveness and necessity of each module within FGDIP. We conduct ablation studies using HotpotQA datasets. The influence of each module on the F1 Score performance is illustrated in the Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Additionally, we explore the framework’s applicability to closed-domain problems and conduct cost analysis to quantify the trade-offs involved in achieving improved reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Multivariate Information Extractor</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The removal of the Extractor module from our model results in significant declines in both F1 scores and accuracy across various difficulty levels, as detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Without the Extractor Module ‣ Appendix C Impact of Module Removal on FGDIP Model Accuracy ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.1 Without the Extractor Module ‣ Appendix C Impact of Module Removal on FGDIP Model Accuracy ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, accuracy declines by 13.16% on easy tasks, 12.68% on medium tasks, and 18.42% on hard tasks, with F1 scores exhibiting similar declines. This sharp decrease highlights that the Extractor module is essential for identifying and integrating relevant information flexibly. Without it, the model adopts a rigid, less effective approach, unable to refocus based on the evolving information landscape, particularly in complex scenarios with multiple interrelated entities. This study underscores the Extractor module’s critical role in avoiding the pitfalls of conventional linear methods by dynamically extracting key information, which enhances the model’s adaptability and accuracy in handling complex, diverse problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Step Evaluator</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess the impact of the Step Evaluator by comparing model performance with and without this module, as accuracy shown in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Without the Step Evaluator Module ‣ Appendix C Impact of Module Removal on FGDIP Model Accuracy ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>. Removing the Step Evaluator leads to slight accuracy gains on easy and medium tasks, increasing by 3.08% and 5.66%, respectively, while decreasing by 1.69% on hard tasks. In theory, removing this module might allow more freedom in exploring solutions and potentially expand the search scope. However, the modest improvement in performance confirms our initial hypothesis: the Step Evaluator is highly effective in maintaining performance while minimizing resource consumption, such as reducing token usage and processing time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While removing the Step Evaluator offers minor gains on simpler tasks, it reduces efficiency and increases resource use. Without it, the model tends to stray into irrelevant information on complex tasks, lowering performance on intricate reasoning steps. This highlights the Step Evaluator’s role in guiding the model effectively and maintaining accuracy across varying task complexities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Answer Evaluator and Error Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this ablation study, we evaluate the impact of removing the Answer Evaluator and Error Analysis from our model, with the detailed results available in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Without the Answer Evaluator Module ‣ Appendix C Impact of Module Removal on FGDIP Model Accuracy ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>. Without these modules, the model’s process concludes immediately after generating an initial answer, forgoing any further validation or correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">F1 scores drop by 9.10% on easy, 7.80% on medium, and 6.44% on hard tasks, with similar declines in accuracy. This decline underscores that the components not only help the model verify the accuracy and relevance of its answers but also facilitate continuous refinement of its reasoning process, leading to more precise and appropriate solutions.
Without these modules, the model’s outputs became less reliable, particularly in tasks requiring deeper reasoning. These components are essential for refining answers and ensuring that mistakes are addressed iteratively, highlighting their fundamental importance in sustaining the model’s performance on complex tasks. Their absence underscores the critical role that continuous evaluation and correction play in achieving accurate results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Applicability to mathematical reasoning Problems</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to its effectiveness in open-domain multi-hop reasoning tasks, our FGDIP framework also demonstrates strong applicability to closed-domain problems. Specifically, we evaluate the performance of FGDIP on a mathematical reasoning task using the Game of 24 dataset, a benchmark for assessing logical and numerical reasoning capabilities, where we use GPT-4 as the inference engine.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.4 Applicability to mathematical reasoning Problems ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, FGDIP, with only <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, achieves an accuracy of 70.73%, surpassing other methods such as Standard, CoT, and ToT. This superior performance highlights the framework’s versatility and robustness, as it can adapt its dynamic reasoning strategies to handle not only the open-domain tasks but also the structured and rule-based challenges presented by closed-domain problems like mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Standard <math id="S5.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k=100)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k=100)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">36.59%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">CoT <math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k=100)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k=100)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">48.78%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">ToT <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k=5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k=5)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">68.29 %</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b">FGDIP <math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k\leq 3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k\leq 3)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S5.T3.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">70.73%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Accuracy comparison on the Game of 24, where k represents best of k samples. We use GPT-4 as the inference engine.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Cost Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FGDIP’s dynamic adjustments and real-time feedback enhance accuracy but increase costs. To quantify impact, we conduct an analysis of the token and time costs associated with various methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ D.1 Token Cost ‣ Appendix D Cost ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, FGDIP only represents a fourfold increase over Reflexion. This contrast is modest compared to the token consumption leap from CoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which sees nearly a thirtyfold increase. Despite FGDIP’s higher token count, the additional resource use is justified by FGDIP’s dynamic search and refinement, crucial for complex reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of time cost, as shown in Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ D.2 Time Cost ‣ Appendix D Cost ‣ Mission Impossible: Feedback-Guided Dynamic Interactive Planning for Improving Reasoning on LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, FGDIP also incurs a higher processing time compared to other methods. Despite these increases, the improvements in accuracy and reasoning strategy make these costs feasible and valuable for solving open-domain problems, where extensive exploration and real-time adjustments are crucial.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study introduces a novel framework for enhancing language agents in open-domain multi-hop reasoning tasks. Using dynamic extraction and adaptive strategies, our approach overcomes traditional fixed-sequence limitations. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements in accuracy and efficiency, particularly on complex datasets like HotpotQA and StrategyQA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Ablation studies confirm the vital roles of FGDIP’s components. The Extractor module efficiently manages and processes complex information, while the Step Evaluator strategically prioritizes the most promising paths, thereby enhancing overall efficiency and decision-making. The Answer Evaluator and Error Analysis ensure accuracy through continuous refinement. Moreover, the framework’s effectiveness extends to closed-domain problems, where it demonstrates adaptability and robust performance, proving its versatility across varied problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework significantly advances language agents by offering a robust, adaptable approach that enhances multi-hop reasoning in complex scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FGDIP’s dynamic adjustments and real-time feedback lead to high costs due to frequent calls to large language models, especially in large-scale open-domain problems, increasing processing times and resource use. The evaluation mechanism, while detailed, relies on predefined rules that may not cover all error types, affecting accuracy and relevance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by the Beijing Natural Science Foundation (IS23059).</p>
</div>
</section>
